WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
Scenario: A caller says they are the principal’s assistant

2
00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:06.785
and demands your class portal password immediately because “the

3
00:00:06.785 --> 00:00:10.177
principal has approved it.” Safest response: Option D: Refuse

4
00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:13.569
to disclose the password and verify the request through

5
00:00:13.569 --> 00:00:16.962
an official contact channel. Authority pressure and urgency are

6
00:00:16.962 --> 00:00:20.354
common social-engineering cues; passwords should not be shared. Option

7
00:00:20.354 --> 00:00:23.746
A is unsafe because Temporary disclosure still compromises the

8
00:00:23.746 --> 00:00:27.138
account and may enable immediate access. Option B is

9
00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:30.531
unsafe because Claims of authority are easy to make

10
00:00:30.531 --> 00:00:33.923
and do not justify password disclosure. Option C is

11
00:00:33.923 --> 00:00:37.692
unsafe because Knowing a name is not independent identity verification.

12
00:00:37.692 --> 00:00:41.085
In practice: Refuse credential disclosure. End the interaction and

13
00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:44.477
confirm any legitimate request through a published contact channel.

14
00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.869
Key takeaway: Authority, familiarity and urgency are persuasion techniques—not

15
00:00:47.869 --> 00:00:49.000
proof of identity.
